  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A particle $P$ of mass $2.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(4-{x}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=1.$",
  "worked_solution": "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $2.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $(4-{x}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=2.5\\cdot (4-{x}^{2})=(10-2.5{x}^{2})\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(10-2.5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[10x-\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(10(1)-\\frac{5}{6}({1}^{3}))-(10(0)-\\frac{5}{6}({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =10-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{6}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
